<bx:output>
<h1>Request &amp; Response</h1>
<p class="bx-docs-lede">The full req.* and res.* API available inside every handler.</p>

<h2>The request object</h2>
<table class="bx-table">
  <tr><th>Property / Method</th><th>Description</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">req.method</code></td><td>Uppercased HTTP method, e.g. <code class="bx-inline">"GET"</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">req.path</code></td><td>Path portion of the URL, no query string</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">req.originalUrl</code></td><td>Path plus query string as received</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">req.query</code></td><td>Struct of parsed query-string parameters. A key sent once is a string; a repeated key is an array — see <a href="##repeated-keys">Repeated keys</a></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">req.params</code></td><td>Struct of captured route params (<code class="bx-inline">:id</code> segments)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">req.body</code></td><td>Parsed request body — populated by <code class="bx-inline">boxExpressJSON()</code>/<code class="bx-inline">boxExpressUrlencoded()</code>/<code class="bx-inline">boxExpressUpload()</code>, empty struct otherwise</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">req.files</code></td><td>Uploaded files, populated by <code class="bx-inline">boxExpressUpload()</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">req.headers</code></td><td>Struct of request headers</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">req.get(name)</code></td><td>Read a single header by name — returns null if absent</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">req.cookies</code></td><td>Struct of parsed cookies — URL-decoded; a value with a malformed escape (<code class="bx-inline">50%</code>) is kept as raw text rather than failing the request</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">req.signedCookies</code></td><td>Signed cookies that passed verification, by name — <code class="bx-inline">false</code> for one that was tampered with. See <a href="##signed-cookies">Signed cookies</a></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">req.ip</code></td><td>Direct TCP peer address by default; prefers <code class="bx-inline">X-Forwarded-For</code> when <code class="bx-inline">app.set("trust proxy", true)</code> — see <a href="/docs/config">Configuration</a></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">req.protocol</code> / <code class="bx-inline">req.secure</code></td><td>Always <code class="bx-inline">"http"</code> / <code class="bx-inline">false</code> — BoxExpress's own <code class="bx-inline">HttpServer</code> never terminates TLS — unless <code class="bx-inline">trust proxy</code> is on and the request carries <code class="bx-inline">X-Forwarded-Proto: https</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">req.hostname</code></td><td>The <code class="bx-inline">Host</code> header (or <code class="bx-inline">X-Forwarded-Host</code> with <code class="bx-inline">trust proxy</code> on) with any <code class="bx-inline">:port</code> stripped</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">req.subdomains</code></td><td>Labels in front of the domain, nearest the domain first — <code class="bx-inline">tobi.ferrets.example.com</code> gives <code class="bx-inline">[ "ferrets", "tobi" ]</code>. <code class="bx-inline">app.set( "subdomain offset", 3 )</code> for a domain like <code class="bx-inline">example.co.uk</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">req.xhr</code></td><td><code class="bx-inline">true</code> when <code class="bx-inline">X-Requested-With</code> is <code class="bx-inline">XMLHttpRequest</code> (jQuery and axios send it; <code class="bx-inline">fetch()</code> doesn't)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">req.baseUrl</code></td><td>The path the current router or path-scoped middleware is mounted at — <code class="bx-inline">""</code> at the top level</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">req.fresh</code> / <code class="bx-inline">req.stale</code></td><td>Whether the client's cached copy still matches the <code class="bx-inline">ETag</code> or <code class="bx-inline">Last-Modified</code> set on the response so far — see <a href="##etags">ETags and 304s</a></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">req.accepts(types)</code></td><td>Whichever of the offered types the <code class="bx-inline">Accept</code> header prefers, or <code class="bx-inline">false</code> — see <a href="##content-negotiation">Content negotiation</a></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">req.acceptsLanguages(langs)</code></td><td>The same against <code class="bx-inline">Accept-Language</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">req.is(types)</code></td><td>The first given type the request's own <code class="bx-inline">Content-Type</code> matches, or <code class="bx-inline">false</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">req.session</code> / <code class="bx-inline">req.sessionID</code></td><td>Populated by <code class="bx-inline">boxExpressSession()</code> — see <a href="/docs/sessions">Sessions</a></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">req.rawExchange()</code></td><td>Escape hatch to the underlying <code class="bx-inline">com.sun.net.httpserver.HttpExchange</code></td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The response object</h2>
<p>Every terminal method funnels through the same internal path that writes headers and body exactly once and closes the exchange — calling two of them on the same response throws.</p>
<table class="bx-table">
  <tr><th>Method</th><th>Description</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">res.send(data)</code></td><td>Sends a string body (or delegates to <code class="bx-inline">res.json()</code> for structs/arrays)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">res.json(data)</code></td><td>Serializes and sends with <code class="bx-inline">Content-Type: application/json</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">res.status(code)</code></td><td>Sets the status code — chainable</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">res.sendStatus(code)</code></td><td>Sends a status with its reason phrase as the body (e.g. <code class="bx-inline">"Not Found"</code>)</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">res.set(name, value)</code> / <code class="bx-inline">res.header(name, value)</code></td><td>Sets an arbitrary response header — aliases of each other. <code class="bx-inline">res.set( {{ ... }} )</code> sets several at once</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">res.get(name)</code></td><td>A response header set so far, or null</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">res.append(name, value)</code></td><td>Adds to a header instead of replacing it</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">res.type(mimeType)</code></td><td>Sets <code class="bx-inline">Content-Type</code> — a full type, or an extension such as <code class="bx-inline">"json"</code> or <code class="bx-inline">".png"</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">res.vary(field)</code></td><td>Adds a request header name to <code class="bx-inline">Vary</code>, once — so a cache keys on every header the response depends on</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">res.location(url)</code></td><td>Sets <code class="bx-inline">Location</code> without ending the response — pair it with <code class="bx-inline">res.status( 201 )</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">res.links( {{ next: url, last: url }} )</code></td><td>Builds a <code class="bx-inline">Link</code> header for paged results</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">res.attachment(filename)</code></td><td><code class="bx-inline">Content-Disposition: attachment</code> for a body you build yourself; the filename's extension sets <code class="bx-inline">Content-Type</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">res.format( [ ... ] )</code></td><td>Runs the handler for the type the client prefers — see <a href="##content-negotiation">Content negotiation</a></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">res.cookie(name, value, options)</code></td><td>Sets a <code class="bx-inline">Set-Cookie</code> header — options: <code class="bx-inline">path</code>, <code class="bx-inline">domain</code>, <code class="bx-inline">maxAge</code>, <code class="bx-inline">expires</code>, <code class="bx-inline">httpOnly</code> (default true), <code class="bx-inline">secure</code>, <code class="bx-inline">sameSite</code>, <code class="bx-inline">signed</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">res.clearCookie(name, options)</code></td><td>Tells the browser to drop a cookie — pass the same <code class="bx-inline">path</code> and <code class="bx-inline">domain</code> it was set with, or the browser keeps it</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">res.redirect(url, code = 302)</code></td><td>Sends a redirect with a caller-supplied status code</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">res.end(data = "")</code></td><td>Ends the response with an optional body, no content-type inference</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">res.sendBytes(bytes, contentType)</code></td><td>Sends raw bytes with an explicit content type, bypassing the string/UTF-8 round trip</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">res.sendFile(path, options)</code></td><td>Sends a file inline, with ETag/Last-Modified 304 support; <code class="bx-inline">options.root</code> sandboxes the path against directory-escape</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">res.download(path, filename)</code></td><td><code class="bx-inline">sendFile()</code> that forces <code class="bx-inline">Content-Disposition: attachment</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">res.render(view, data)</code></td><td>Renders a view — see <a href="/docs/views">Views &amp; Templates</a></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">res.dump(data)</code></td><td>Sends BoxLang's rich, collapsible <code class="bx-inline">dump()</code> HTML view of a variable as the response — useful for quick debugging routes</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Examples</h2>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">bxs</span><code>app.get( "/headers-demo", ( req, res ) => {
    res.header( "X-Powered-By", "BoxLang Express" )
       .type( "text/plain" )
       .send( "check the headers" )
} )

app.get( "/whoami", ( req, res ) => {
    res.json( { ip: req.ip, userAgent: req.get( "User-Agent" ) } )
} )

app.get( "/moved", ( req, res ) => {
    res.redirect( "/new-location", 301 )
} )

app.get( "/debug", ( req, res ) => {
    res.dump( req.query )
} )</code></pre>

<h2 id="content-negotiation">Content negotiation</h2>
<p>One route can serve a browser and an API client. <code class="bx-inline">res.format()</code> reads the request's <code class="bx-inline">Accept</code> header, runs the handler for the type it prefers, sets <code class="bx-inline">Content-Type</code> to match and adds <code class="bx-inline">Accept</code> to <code class="bx-inline">Vary</code>. If nothing matches and there's no <code class="bx-inline">default</code> handler, the response is <code class="bx-inline">406 Not Acceptable</code>.</p>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">bxs</span><code>demo = boxExpressRouter()

demo.get( "/negotiate", ( req, res ) => {
    var info = {
        accepts: req.accepts( "html", "json" ),
        language: req.acceptsLanguages( "en", "fr", "de" ),
        xhr: req.xhr,
        baseUrl: req.baseUrl,
        path: req.path,
        originalUrl: req.originalUrl
    }
    res.format( [
        html: () => res.send( "&lt;pre&gt;" & encodeForHTML( JSONSerialize( info ) ) & "&lt;/pre&gt;" ),
        json: () => res.json( info )
    ] )
} )

app.use( "/docs/request-response/demo", demo )</code></pre>
<p>That router is mounted in this site. <a href="/docs/request-response/demo/negotiate">Open it in your browser</a> and you get the HTML handler; ask for JSON and the same URL answers differently:</p>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">bash</span><code>curl -i -H "Accept: application/json" -H "Accept-Language: de, fr;q=0.5" \
  http://localhost:3003/docs/request-response/demo/negotiate

curl -i -H "Accept: image/png" http://localhost:3003/docs/request-response/demo/negotiate   ## 406</code></pre>
<p><code class="bx-inline">req.baseUrl</code> in the output is <code class="bx-inline">/docs/request-response/demo</code> — where the router is mounted — while <code class="bx-inline">req.path</code> is just <code class="bx-inline">/negotiate</code>.</p>
<div class="bx-callout bx-warn">
  <strong>Pass res.format() an ordered struct</strong>
  When a client has no preference (<code class="bx-inline">Accept: */*</code>, or no header at all — curl's default) the first key wins. A <code class="bx-inline">[ ... ]</code> struct keeps its keys in the order you wrote them; a plain <code class="bx-inline">{{ ... }}</code> struct doesn't, so which handler runs would be arbitrary.
</div>
<p><code class="bx-inline">req.accepts()</code> is the same decision without the dispatch, for when a <code class="bx-inline">switch</code> reads better. <code class="bx-inline">req.is()</code> looks the other way — at what the client <em>sent</em>:</p>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">bxs</span><code>app.post( "/hook", ( req, res ) => {
    if ( !req.is( "json", "+json" ) ) {   // application/json, application/vnd.api+json, ...
        return res.sendStatus( 415 )
    }
    res.sendStatus( 204 )
} )</code></pre>

<h2 id="repeated-keys">Repeated keys in query strings and forms</h2>
<p><code class="bx-inline">req.query</code> follows Express 5's default parser. A key that appears once is a string. A key that repeats — a multi-select, checkboxes sharing a name, a filter UI — is an array, in the order sent. <code class="bx-inline">boxExpressUrlencoded()</code> reads form bodies the same way.</p>
<table class="bx-table">
  <tr><th>Request</th><th><code class="bx-inline">req.query</code></th></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">?tag=a</code></td><td><code class="bx-inline">{ tag: "a" }</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">?tag=a&amp;tag=b</code></td><td><code class="bx-inline">{ tag: [ "a", "b" ] }</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">?tag[]=a&amp;tag[]=b</code></td><td><code class="bx-inline">{ "tag[]": [ "a", "b" ] }</code> — brackets are not parsed</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">?Tag=a&amp;tag=b</code></td><td><code class="bx-inline">{ Tag: [ "a", "b" ] }</code> — struct keys are case-insensitive</td></tr>
</table>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">bxs</span><code>negotiationDemo.get( "/tags", ( req, res ) => {   // mounted at /docs/request-response/demo
    var tags = req.query.tag ?: []
    if ( !isArray( tags ) ) {
        tags = [ tags ]
    }
    res.json( { query: req.query, tags: tags } )
} )</code></pre>
<p>Try it with <a href="/docs/request-response/demo/tags?tag=boxlang">one tag</a> and with <a href="/docs/request-response/demo/tags?tag=boxlang&amp;tag=express&amp;tag=undertow&amp;page=2">three</a>.</p>
<div class="bx-callout bx-warn">
  <strong>Any client can repeat a key</strong>
  A value you expect to be a string can arrive as an array, and string code (<code class="bx-inline">len()</code>, <code class="bx-inline">&amp;</code>, <code class="bx-inline">trim()</code>) then throws. Normalise it as above, or declare it with <a href="/docs/middleware##validation">boxExpressValidate()</a>: a <code class="bx-inline">string</code> or <code class="bx-inline">integer</code> field answers <code class="bx-inline">400</code> for an array, and an <code class="bx-inline">array</code> field accepts one value or several.
</div>

<h2 id="etags">ETags and 304s</h2>
<p>With <code class="bx-inline">app.set( "etag", "weak" )</code>, <code class="bx-inline">res.send()</code>, <code class="bx-inline">res.json()</code>, <code class="bx-inline">res.render()</code> and <code class="bx-inline">res.sendBytes()</code> tag a <code class="bx-inline">GET</code> or <code class="bx-inline">HEAD</code> response with a hash of its body. A client that sends the tag back in <code class="bx-inline">If-None-Match</code> gets an empty <code class="bx-inline">304 Not Modified</code> instead of the body again. It is off by default; this site turns it on.</p>
<table class="bx-table">
  <tr><th><code class="bx-inline">app.set( "etag", ... )</code></th><th>Result</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">false</code> (default)</td><td>No <code class="bx-inline">ETag</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">"weak"</code> or <code class="bx-inline">true</code></td><td><code class="bx-inline">W/"&lt;length&gt;-&lt;SHA-1&gt;"</code>, the same format Express uses</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">"strong"</code></td><td>The same without <code class="bx-inline">W/</code></td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">( bodyBytes, encoding ) => tag</code></td><td>Your own tag</td></tr>
</table>
<p><a href="/docs/request-response/demo/etag">This route</a> always sends the same JSON. Reload it with dev tools open and the second response is a <code class="bx-inline">304</code>; from a terminal:</p>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">bash</span><code>curl -i http://localhost:3003/docs/request-response/demo/etag                              ## 200, with an ETag header
curl -i -H 'If-None-Match: &lt;that ETag&gt;' http://localhost:3003/docs/request-response/demo/etag   ## 304, no body
curl -i -H 'If-None-Match: &lt;that ETag&gt;' -H 'Cache-Control: no-cache' http://localhost:3003/docs/request-response/demo/etag   ## 200 again</code></pre>
<p>The handler still runs — the tag is a hash of what it built — so this saves bandwidth, not work. To skip the work, set a validator that is cheap to compute and check <code class="bx-inline">req.fresh</code> first:</p>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">bxs</span><code>app.get( "/reports/:id", ( req, res ) => {
    res.set( "ETag", '"report-' &amp; reports.versionOf( req.params.id ) &amp; '"' )   // cheap
    if ( req.fresh ) {
        return res.status( 304 ).end()
    }
    res.json( reports.build( req.params.id ) )                                   // expensive
} )</code></pre>
<p>A response that carries its own <code class="bx-inline">ETag</code> or <code class="bx-inline">Last-Modified</code> gets the automatic <code class="bx-inline">304</code> whether or not the <code class="bx-inline">etag</code> setting is on. Files sent with <code class="bx-inline">res.sendFile()</code> or the static middleware have always had their own tags and are not affected.</p>

<h2 id="signed-cookies">Signed cookies</h2>
<p>A cookie is whatever the browser sends back, and a user can edit theirs. A signed cookie carries an HMAC of its own value, so an edited one is rejected when it returns. Set a secret once, then pass <code class="bx-inline">signed: true</code>:</p>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">bxs</span><code>app.set( "cookie secret", server.system.environment.COOKIE_SECRET ?: "dev-only-secret" )

negotiationDemo.get( "/signed-cookie", ( req, res ) => {   // mounted at /docs/request-response/demo
    var previous = req.signedCookies.demo_visits ?: 0
    var tampered = ( previous === false )
    var visits = tampered ? 1 : previous + 1
    res.cookie( "demo_visits", visits, { signed: true, path: "/docs/request-response/demo" } )
    res.json( { visits: visits, tampered: tampered } )
} )</code></pre>
<p><a href="/docs/request-response/demo/signed-cookie">Open it</a> and reload a few times to watch the count rise. Then open your browser's dev tools, change a digit in the <code class="bx-inline">demo_visits</code> cookie, and reload: the count resets and <code class="bx-inline">tampered</code> is <code class="bx-inline">true</code>. The same thing from a terminal:</p>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">bash</span><code>curl -s -c jar.txt http://localhost:3003/docs/request-response/demo/signed-cookie   ## {"visits":1,"tampered":false}
curl -s -b jar.txt http://localhost:3003/docs/request-response/demo/signed-cookie   ## {"visits":2,"tampered":false}
curl -s -b "demo_visits=s%3A99.forged" http://localhost:3003/docs/request-response/demo/signed-cookie   ## {"visits":1,"tampered":true}</code></pre>
<p><code class="bx-inline">req.signedCookies</code> holds the original value of each signed cookie, or <code class="bx-inline">false</code> for one whose signature doesn't check out. Either way a signed cookie is removed from <code class="bx-inline">req.cookies</code>, so a forged value can't be read there by mistake. Test for tampering with <code class="bx-inline">=== false</code> — a plain <code class="bx-inline">==</code> would also match a genuine value of <code class="bx-inline">"0"</code> or <code class="bx-inline">"no"</code>.</p>
<div class="bx-callout bx-warn">
  <strong>Signed is not encrypted</strong>
  Anyone holding the cookie can read its value; the signature only proves this server issued it. Don't put secrets in one, and keep anything that must be revocable in the <a href="/docs/sessions">session</a> instead. To change the secret without logging everyone out, pass an array — <code class="bx-inline">app.set( "cookie secret", [ newSecret, oldSecret ] )</code> signs with the first and accepts both.
</div>

<h2>Tips &amp; Tricks: res.dump()</h2>
<p><code class="bx-inline">res.dump()</code> is BoxLang's own rich, interactive <code class="bx-inline">dump()</code> — the same one you'd get from a CLI script — rendered as the HTTP response instead of printed to the console. A few things worth knowing:</p>

<h3>It's a terminal method — dump one thing</h3>
<p>Like <code class="bx-inline">res.send()</code>/<code class="bx-inline">res.json()</code>, <code class="bx-inline">res.dump()</code> writes the response and closes the exchange — nothing can be sent after it. To inspect several things at once, wrap them in a single struct rather than calling it more than once:</p>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">bxs</span><code>app.get( "/debug", ( req, res ) => {
    res.dump( {
        query: req.query,
        params: req.params,
        headers: req.headers,
        session: req.session
    } )
} )</code></pre>

<h3>It's click-to-expand</h3>
<p>The rendered dump isn't static markup — struct/array row headers are clickable (and keyboard-focusable) to toggle that branch open or closed, so a large dump doesn't have to be read top-to-bottom. Handy for a deeply nested <code class="bx-inline">req.session</code> or a big query result.</p>

<h3>Gate debug routes behind your env setting</h3>
<p>A dump can expose more than you'd want a stranger to see — internal struct shapes, full session contents, request headers. Guard debug-only routes the same way the framework's own error pages decide whether to reveal a real error message (see <a href="/docs/errors">Error Handling</a>):</p>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">bxs</span><code>app.get( "/debug/session", ( req, res, next ) => {
    if ( app.getSetting( "env" ) != "development" ) {
        next() // fall through to the normal 404 — don't reveal the route exists
        return
    }
    res.dump( req.session )
} )</code></pre>

<h3>Reach for more control when you need it</h3>
<p><code class="bx-inline">res.dump()</code> is a thin convenience wrapper — it only forwards the value to dump, not the underlying <code class="bx-inline">dump()</code> BIF's other options. The BIF itself supports several worth knowing about when you need more than the default view:</p>
<table class="bx-table">
  <tr><th>Argument</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">label</code></td><td>Adds a titled banner above the dump — useful to tell two dumps apart on the same page</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">expand = false</code></td><td>Renders every branch collapsed by default instead of open — the single biggest win for a large struct or query result</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code class="bx-inline">top = n</code></td><td>Limits how many levels deep the dump descends before truncating — shrinks the payload for deeply nested data</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Since <code class="bx-inline">res.dump()</code> doesn't expose these, write the same temp-file round trip it uses internally in your own route when you need them — <code class="bx-inline">dump()</code>'s HTML output only ever reaches the process console under BoxLang Express's CLI-based HTTP server, so routing it through a temp file and reading it back is what makes it show up in the response at all:</p>
<pre class="bx-code"><span class="bx-code-label">bxs</span><code>app.get( "/debug/session", ( req, res ) => {
    var tmpFile = getTempDirectory() & "dump-" & createUUID() & ".html"
    dump(
        var = req.session,
        format = "html",
        output = tmpFile,
        label = "Session State",
        expand = false,
        top = 3
    )
    var dumpHtml = fileRead( tmpFile )
    fileDelete( tmpFile )
    res.type( "text/html; charset=utf-8" ).send( dumpHtml )
} )</code></pre>

<h3>It's fine for dev, not for hot paths</h3>
<p>Both the temp-file round trip and the HTML dump renderer itself have real cost compared to <code class="bx-inline">res.json()</code>. That's a non-issue for an occasional debug route hit by a developer, but it's not something to leave wired into a frequently-hit production endpoint.</p>

